{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"In the compensation example (30 V, 2 Ω, then 6 Ω in parallel with a 3 Ω branch carrying 5 A), the 3 Ω becomes 2 Ω instead. Find its new current by compensation.",say:'Question two. In the compensation example, the three ohm branch becomes two ohms instead. Find its new current.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'In the compensation'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',pause:.8,lv:'A',n:2,q:"In the compensation example (30 V, 2 Ω, then 6 Ω in parallel with a 3 Ω branch carrying 5 A), the 3 Ω becomes 2 Ω instead. Find its new current by compensation.",say:`Delta R is minus one ohm, so the source is minus five volts: it now aids the current. Over three point five ohms it adds one point four two nine amps: six point four two nine in all. Check: it agrees.`,cues:{fk:'Delta R is',k1:'Delta R is',k1b:'it now aids',k2:'Over three point',k2b:'six point four',k3:'Check: it',k3b:'agrees'},calc:{f:[`${CT_sub('V','c')} = IΔR`,`ΔI = ${CT_sub('V','c')} / (R + ΔR + ${CT_sub('R','th')})`],hint:`${CT_sub('R','th')} = 2 ∥ 6 = 1.5 Ω`,fk:'fk',steps:[["Source",["ΔR = −1 Ω:  " + `${CT_sub('V','c')} = 5 × (−1) = −5 V`,`${CT_sub('V','c')} = −5 V: it now aids I`],["k1","k1b"]],["Change",["ΔI = 5 / (2 + 1.5) = 1.429 A","new I = 5 + 1.429 = 6.429 A"],["k2","k2b"]],["Check",["30 / (2 + 6 ∥ 2) = 8.571 A","8.571 × 6/8 = 6.429 A ✓"],["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Show that an ammeter's percentage error depends on the ratio of its resistance to the resistance round the measured loop.",say:"Question three. Show that an ammeter's percentage error depends on its resistance, compared with the resistance around the measured loop.",cues:{k:'Question three',q:'Show that an'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Show that an ammeter's percentage error depends on the ratio of its resistance to the resistance round the measured loop.",say:`The reading is the true current times the loop without the meter, over the loop with it. So the error is the meter's resistance over the whole loop. Half over five: ten percent. A zero point zero five ohm meter: about one point one percent.`,cues:{a1:'The reading is',a1b:'So the error',a2:'Half over five',a3:'A zero point'},al:[['Reading = I(Rth + R)/(Rth + R + RA)','a1'],['Error = RA/(Rth + R + RA) of the true value','a1b'],["Worked example 2: 0.5/5 = 10 %",'a2'],["A 0.05 Ω meter: 0.05/4.55 = 1.1 %",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('fade',tk(c,'a1'),CT_L(70,440,590,440,CT_AX,2)+CT_L(70,440,70,70,CT_AX,2)+[0,5,10,15,20].map(e=>CT_L(64,CT_ey(e),70,CT_ey(e),CT_MUT,2)+CT_T(58,CT_ey(e)+7,e+' %',{fs:20,a:'end',fill:CT_MUT})).join('')+[0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1].map(r=>CT_L(CT_ex(r),440,CT_ex(r),446,CT_MUT,2)+CT_T(CT_ex(r),472,String(r),{fs:20,a:'middle',fill:CT_MUT})).join('')+CT_T(330,504,`${CT_sub('R','A')} (Ω), loop without the meter 4.5 Ω`,{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:CT_MUT})+CT_poly([...Array(41)].map((_,i)=>[CT_ex(i/40),CT_ey(CT_err(i/40))]),BLU,5)+CT_T(110,60,`error = ${CT_sub('R','A')} / (4.5 + ${CT_sub('R','A')})`,{fs:24,fw:800,fill:BLU}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),`<circle cx="${CT_ex(0.5)}" cy="${CT_ey(CT_err(0.5))}" r="10" fill="${ORG}"/>`+CT_T(CT_ex(0.5)+18,CT_ey(CT_err(0.5))+30,'0.5 Ω: 10 %',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a3'),`<circle cx="${CT_ex(0.05)}" cy="${CT_ey(CT_err(0.05))}" r="10" fill="${GRN}"/>`+CT_T(CT_ex(0.05)+18,CT_ey(CT_err(0.05))-16,'0.05 Ω: 1.1 %',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:GRN}))}`},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:`Your free notes are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the compensation network and the load's ${P('Thevenin')} view.`,cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the compensation'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["The two chapters", "Compensation theorem; Applying network theorems to AC circuits"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["The compensation network", "The 3 Ω branch carrying 5 A becomes 4 Ω; with the 30 V source shorted, a 5 V source in that branch drives exactly the changes: 0.909 A in it, +0.227 A in the 6 Ω."], ["The AC circuit and its Thevenin view", "Mesh currents 2 + j1 A and 1 − j2 A; the load sees 10 − j10 V behind 2 − j2 Ω, and the conjugate load 2 + j2 Ω takes 25 W against 20 W."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku (Fundamentals of Electric Circuits); William H. Hayt, Jack E. Kemmerly and Steven M. Durbin (Engineering Circuit Analysis); James W. Nilsson and Susan A. Riedel (Electric Circuits); M. E. Van Valkenburg (Network Analysis)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:`So remember: a change is a source opposing the old current, ${P('AC')} theorems run on phasors, and maximum power wants the conjugate. Next time, sinusoids and their ${P('RMS')} values. See you there!`,cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'a change is',w2:'theorems run on',w3:'and maximum power',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['CHANGE = SOURCE IΔZ','BLU','w1'],['AC: SAME METHODS, PHASORS','ORG','w2'],['MAX POWER: CONJUGATE LOAD','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Sinusoids: RMS and average values, form factor", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
